            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 25-year-old man presents with a contaminated forearm laceration. You have controlled the haemorrhage with direct pressure. What is the next correct step in wound toileting?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Debride all devitalised tissue immediately</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Debridement follows irrigation. The correct order is haemostasis → irrigation → debridement. Irrigating first washes out loose contaminants before surgical debridement.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Irrigate the wound copiously with normal saline</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>After achieving haemostasis, copious saline irrigation removes dirt and debris. Debridement of devitalised tissue follows, completing wound toileting before local anaesthesia and closure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Infiltrate local anaesthetic around the wound edges</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Local anaesthetic infiltration comes after wound toileting is complete. The sequence is haemostasis, then irrigation, then debridement — only after these do you anaesthetise and close.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Begin suturing the wound edges closed</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Suturing is the final step, performed only after wound toileting (haemostasis → irrigation → debridement) and adequate local anaesthesia. Closing before cleaning risks sealing in debris and bacteria.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The wound toileting sequence is haemostasis → irrigation → debridement. After bleeding is controlled, copious saline irrigation clears contaminants before you debride devitalised tissue."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. After haemostasis, copious saline irrigation is the next step in wound toileting, followed by debridement of devitalised tissue."
                }
              ],
