          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old woman presents with a contaminated thigh laceration sustained eight hours ago. The wound edges are viable. What is the most appropriate wound closure strategy?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediate primary closure with interrupted sutures</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Closing a contaminated, late-presenting wound immediately seals bacteria inside and invites wound infection. The viable tissue makes delayed primary closure the better option.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Delayed primary closure: clean and debride now, pack open, and suture at 3–5 days</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A contaminated wound seen late but with viable tissue is best managed by delayed primary closure — clean and debride now, pack it open, and return to suture at 3–5 days once you are confident infection has not developed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Leave the wound entirely open for healing by secondary intention</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Secondary intention is reserved for heavy contamination with significant tissue loss, established infection, or abscess cavities. Since the tissue here is viable, delayed primary closure achieves faster healing with a better scar.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Close with adhesive strips and discharge without planned follow-up</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adhesive strips alone are inadequate for a deep contaminated wound, and omitting follow-up is unsafe. This wound needs debridement, packing, and planned reassessment for delayed closure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The closure decision integrates contamination, timing, and tissue viability. A contaminated wound seen eight hours late with viable edges calls for delayed primary closure — never immediate closure, which traps bacteria."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Delayed primary closure is the right strategy for a contaminated, late-presenting wound with viable tissue — clean now, close in 3–5 days."
                }
              ],
